A||||16||PaUL||Moving battery to the front
Z||000000||PaUL||04-30-99||02:24 PM||||Hi, where I am it rains a lot and my batt terms get corroded very quickly. Ive been thinking about moving the batt to the front where the spare tire is. Ive heard of a few people doing this and was wondering how this is done. Does a long heavy gauge cable need to be routed through the center tunnel? Do they use a separate solenoid/switch as in most Ford cars? Where does the ground go, the steel frame in the front or do you have run a separate ground cable through the tunnel attached to the neg term on the batt? 
Z||000001||Motorhead||04-30-99||06:07 PM||||West coast Fieros has a kit complete with tray and cables for moving the battery to the front compartment, underneath the spare tire. This conversion is on my list of mods.<p>My purpose for doing this is to gain space for another modification I'm working on. Also provides a little weight transfer toward the nose.<p>Haven't ordered the kit yet, but I'm planning to in the next week or two. Just exceeded the budget on some other parts.<p>Funny how I seem to keep alianating the battery from the engine, the Z-Car battery migrated to the rear of the car to put some weight over the rear end. Still seeking that magic 50/50 front/rear balance I guess!<p>If you decide to do this and beat me to the punch let me know how it turns out, I'll do the same. <p>
Z||000002||PaUL||04-30-99||06:20 PM||||hey Motorhead,<br>that battery reloc kit for WestCoast sure costs a lot, $120 just for the tray? wow!!<p>I cant see why you just cant use one of those plastic battery boxes for boats. Actually the box is the easy part, the hard part is the wiring. All Fords have a separate solenoid/switch, the heavy gauge cable going to the starter is not always live, like in most GM products. On Ford starters, there is only 1 heavy cable connection and nothing else. I was thinking about rigging a similar kind of connection using a remote solenoid. The purple wire normally going to the starter would instead go to the solenoid. I dont know where to run the heavy cable, is center tunnel the best place?
Z||000003||theogre||04-30-99||08:01 PM||||if all you are worried about is corrostion, drop some motor oil on the terminals after you connect them. I use a top post battery, is was convenient at the time, and I only have problems if I forget to oil the posts after any work that requires disconecting it.<p>you can also make a cover out of alomst any thickish plastic sheet. all you need is to keep off the majority of the water.<p>motor oil works better than grease I think... it soaks into any open poors and seals out the water and air, grease won't do that as well.
Z||000004||rcn491||04-30-99||08:55 PM||||I just relocated mine to the luggage<br>compartment. The Neg. lead should be<br>connected to the engine block. You also<br>need to extend the smaller Pos. lead <br>which supplies charging current.<br>
Z||000005||PaUL||04-30-99||09:08 PM||||relocating the batt to the luggage compartment would be easy, Im talking about moving it all the way to the front. If I did this and connected the neg term to the metal frame in the front with no direct ground to the engine, would the starter still work?<p>Youve all seen the remote type of solenoid Im referring to havent you? I would still need a live pos. wire coming from the batt to the electrical juction block in the rear for charging. <p>And Ogre, Ill try that motor oil on the terms. I got the side terms and mine has crystalized deposits visible on the pos.
Z||000006||ezsurf||04-30-99||10:18 PM||||PaUL,<br>You can get battery terminal protector in a spray can at any Auto Zone.  You can also use battery terminal washers (I call them donuts) they are coated in a special chemical to prevent corrosion. One is green and one is red.   I have found that both work just fine.  Although without relocating the battery, you will probably want to use the washers because there is no chance they can wash off.  I've heard of using grease, but not oil.  But, I'm sure oil works great, as grease is just a thick oil anyway.  
Z||000007||rcn491||04-30-99||11:49 PM||||The starter might work if you had a<br>big ground wire from the engine to the<br>frame and good connections on the frame.<br>Remember the same amount of electric<br>current has to come out of the starter<br>as goes in .
Z||000008||Patrick||05-01-99||01:00 AM||||<br>Regarding the battery terminals, I've got you all beat.:) <p>I use an old jar of <b>Vaseline Petroleum Jelly</b>. It's easier to put on than oil because you have some control over its application, and it has a lower melting temperature than automotive grease, so it melts real easy and <b>then</b> flows into every crack and crevice. Apply it liberally with your finger all over the terminals and never worry about battery corrosion again.<p>This is a hell of a lot easier than moving the battery up front just to keep it dry.:)<p>
Z||000009||Motorhead||05-01-99||06:32 PM||||Your'e right about cost of batt. box Paul, pretty pricey, but I think it would make for a more profesional looking installation.<br>Those boat battery boxes are pretty flimsy, and look like "BOAT" battery boxes.<p>As for wiring, the engine is grounded to the chassis isn't it?  <br>Wouldn't grounding the battery to a substantial chassis member be sufficiant, or am I all wet in my understanding of electricity?<p><br>
Z||000010||theogre||05-01-99||09:52 PM||||the stock engine grounding strap Will Not be suficient for a relocated batery. Grounding thru the frame is perfectly fine, but the engine ground strap must be upgraded.<p>GM vehicals don't need a "ford type" solinoid. GM intigrates it onto the starter.<p>to move the battery,,, at the least,,, you need a ground for the bat to the frame, an improved engine ground, a very long + lead, and a box to fit the batery cut in under the spare tire. The + lead from the battery to the terminal block will also need to be altered. You can conect this to the starter main + terminal where the batery connects.<p>because wire runs for + are sginifigantly longer you must also use a heavier gauge wire. I.E. if the stock wire is #4 then consider #2-#0 or you will likely have currnet lose problems caused by the wire itself. 
Z||000011||Motorhead||05-02-99||03:43 AM||||Thanks Ogre, I see what you mean about the engine ground strap.
Z||000012||PaUL||05-02-99||01:32 PM||||Ive seen batt reloc kits from Summit and Jegs. These are for moving the batt from the front to the rear and come with tray and cables. In the Fiero, youd be moving the batt from the rear to the front.
Z||000013||Pretender||05-03-99||12:56 AM||||My friend bought the relocating kit from summit., comes w/ everything you need(for $42.95).....i imagine that making a tray wouldn't be that hard., piece of sheet-metal cut and bent to the right dimensions and angles with a few braces under-neith, screwed into place, using the factory hold-down piece from the tray.
Z||000014||Carrolles||05-04-99||11:40 AM||||When a battery is charging and discharging it produces Hydrogen Gas.  This is a very bad actor when put in a confined space that has electrical contacts.<p>Be sure to install a ventilation kit made for this type installation to carry the Hydrogen gas to the outside of the car.  You have several sources of spark in the forward storage compartment. <p>PLEASE DO IT RIGHT AND LIVE TO ENJOY IT!<p>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000015||Motorhead||05-04-99||03:12 PM||||Great advice Carroll, thanks for the input!
Z||000016||PaUL||05-04-99||07:14 PM||||Ive had a battery explode on me before, well not completely but it did spray acid all over my engine. This was not a Fiero
